  "name": "Nouns and gender: le or la?",
  "level": "A1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "variety": "fr-FR",
  "subtitle": "Every French noun is masculine or feminine, even a table or a street. Endings help: -tion and -té are usually feminine (la station, la liberté), -ment and -age are usually masculine (le logement, le village). Learn each noun with le or la, or with un or une.",
  "source": "icanspeak French original text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-11-08)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "le / la / l'", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "noun", "variant": "verb" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">le</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">village</span> · <span class=\"vp-prep\">la</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">station</span> · <span class=\"vp-prep\">un</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">gâteau</span> · <span class=\"vp-prep\">une</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">baguette</span>",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "These endings are usually <strong>feminine</strong>: <strong>-tion, -sion, -té, -ette</strong>: <em>la station, la télévision, la liberté, la baguette</em>.",
        "tag": "Feminine endings"
      },
      {
        "html": "These endings are usually <strong>masculine</strong>: <strong>-ment, -age, -eau</strong>: <em>le logement, le village, le gâteau, le bureau</em>.",
        "tag": "Masculine endings"
      },
      {
        "html": "Learn these exceptions early: <em><strong>la</strong> page, <strong>la</strong> plage, <strong>l'</strong>eau (feminine), <strong>la</strong> main, <strong>la</strong> photo</em> and <em><strong>le</strong> problème, <strong>le</strong> système</em>.",
        "tag": "Exceptions"
      },
      {
        "html": "Before a vowel or a silent h, <em>le</em> and <em>la</em> both become <strong>l'</strong>: <em>l'école, l'hôpital</em>. Then the article does not show the gender. Learn the noun with <strong>un</strong> or <strong>une</strong>: <em>une école, un hôpital</em>.",
        "tag": "l' hides the gender"
      },
      {
        "html": "For people, the word often changes: <em>un voisin → une voisine, un infirmier → une infirmière</em>. Some words stay the same and only the article changes: <em>un élève, une élève</em>; <em>un médecin, une médecin</em>.",
        "tag": "People"
      },
      {
        "html": "Names of people and places take a capital letter: <em>Inès, Lyon, la France</em>. Days, months and languages take a small letter: <em>lundi, mai, le français</em>. So does a nationality used as an adjective: <em>elle est française</em>.",
        "tag": "Small letters"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>le page</em> → ✓ <em>la page</em>. ✗ <em>la problème</em> → ✓ <em>le problème</em>. ✗ <em>J'apprends le Français</em> → ✓ <em>J'apprends le français</em>. Next: <a href=\"/articles\">Articles</a> and <a href=\"/pluriels\">Plurals</a>.",
